def L(n,page,heading,title,body,formula=''):
 data['chapters'][11+n]['lessons'].append(dict(title=title,body=body,formula=formula,source=f'ied-{n}-{page:02}',kind='Concept & answer points',textbookHeading=heading))
L(1,11,'1.1 Overall Features of Indian Economy','Before British rule and the colonial transformation','Before British rule, India had a prosperous handicraft sector, particularly cotton and silk textiles, metal work and precious stones. Agriculture was the main occupation. Colonial policies transformed India into a supplier of raw materials and a market for British manufactured goods. Their principal purpose was to serve Britain’s economic interests, rather than develop India.\n\nFeatures on the eve of independence: stagnant economy; backward agriculture; depleted industrial base; adverse demographic conditions; dependence on foreign trade serving colonial interests; and inadequate infrastructure. Explain each feature rather than writing only a list.')
L(1,10,'1.2 Agricultural Sector on the Eve of Independence','Why agriculture stagnated','Low productivity and stagnation characterised agriculture even though it supported most of the population. The main reasons were: exploitative land settlement; low levels of technology; lack of irrigation; negligible use of fertilisers; and insufficient investment.\n\nUnder the zamindari system, intermediaries collected rent. High rents and insecurity of tenure discouraged cultivators from improving land; zamindars concentrated on collecting rent rather than raising productivity. Distinguish a large share of workers in farming from high agricultural productivity: they can coexist with poverty.')
L(1,9,'1.2 Agricultural Sector · Commercialisation of Agriculture','Commercialisation, subsistence and vulnerability','Commercialisation of agriculture means producing crops for sale in the market rather than for self-consumption. Colonial demand encouraged cash crops such as cotton and jute. Farmers often grew them under pressure and did not necessarily receive the gains. The shift could reduce food-crop cultivation and expose farmers to market fluctuations.\n\nSubsistence agriculture produces primarily for the family’s own consumption. The key distinction is the purpose of production; commercialisation alone does not prove higher productivity or better living standards.')
L(1,8,'1.3.1 Systematic De-industrialisation','Decline of handicrafts: causes and consequences','De-industrialisation refers to the decline of India’s handicraft industry without a corresponding expansion of modern industry. Discriminatory tariff policy favoured British manufactures; machine-made imports competed with Indian handicrafts; and the disappearance of royal courts weakened demand for traditional products.\n\nConsequences: unemployment among artisans, greater pressure on agriculture, loss of traditional skills and a growing market for British manufactured goods. The colonial objective was twofold: obtain cheap raw materials from India and sell British industrial products here.')
L(1,7,'1.3.2 Growth of Modern Industry during 1850–1947','A limited modern industrial base','Modern industry developed slowly: cotton textile mills were concentrated in western India, especially Bombay; jute mills in Bengal were largely foreign-owned. Iron and steel emerged later, including TISCO in 1907. Capital-goods industries remained inadequate, and the public sector was confined mainly to railways, power generation, communications, ports and some departmental undertakings.\n\nA few successful industries do not overturn the overall conclusion: industrial growth was too limited to absorb displaced artisans or transform the economy.')
L(1,7,'1.4 Foreign Trade on the Eve of Independence','Foreign trade and the drain of wealth','India exported primary products and imported finished consumer goods and capital goods. Britain exercised substantial control over India’s trade; other trading partners included China, Ceylon and Persia. The opening of the Suez Canal reduced transport costs and reinforced British control.\n\nIndia often generated an export surplus, but it did not translate into domestic development. The surplus financed British administrative expenses, war expenditure and other payments abroad. This is the drain of Indian wealth. An export surplus is not automatically evidence that the domestic population benefited.')
L(1,5,'1.5 Demographic Profile','Population conditions and the year 1921','The demographic profile showed high birth and death rates, high infant mortality, low life expectancy, low literacy and inadequate public-health facilities. Female literacy was especially low. Poverty, poor sanitation and disease contributed to these conditions.\n\n1921 is described as the Year of the Great Divide: before it, population growth was irregular and the economy was in the first stage of demographic transition; afterwards, population growth became more sustained as death rates declined while birth rates remained high. When using numerical indicators, quote the year attached to the textbook’s table; historical figures are not current data.')
L(1,4,'1.6 Occupational Structure','Occupational imbalance','Occupational structure means the distribution of the working population across different occupations or sectors. Agriculture dominated employment, while manufacturing and services absorbed much smaller shares. Regional differences existed: some regions experienced a movement away from agriculture, while others became more dependent on it.\n\nThe imbalance reflected inadequate industrial development and disguised pressure on land. Do not substitute the sectoral distribution of GDP for the sectoral distribution of workers: these measure different things.')
L(1,3,'1.7 Infrastructure · 1.7.1 Positive Impact','Railways and other infrastructure: purpose and effects','Railways, roads, ports, posts and telegraphs developed primarily to serve colonial administration, move troops, transport raw materials and expand the market for British goods. Roads were inadequate, especially in rural areas. Railways also encouraged commercialisation of agriculture and movement of goods and people.\n\nA balanced answer recognises unintended benefits—transport links, communication and national integration—while retaining the central qualification: colonial economic interests determined their design and priorities. Development of an asset does not establish that its original purpose was Indian welfare.')
L(2,15,'2.1 Economic Systems','Capitalist, socialist and mixed economies','A capitalist economy relies on private ownership and market forces; production responds mainly to purchasing power and profitability. A socialist economy assigns ownership and allocation mainly to the government, aiming to meet social needs. A mixed economy combines public and private sectors.\n\nIndia adopted a mixed economy: the public sector took the leading role in strategic and basic industries, while regulated private enterprise operated alongside it. The choice reflected limited private capital, the need for infrastructure and the objectives of growth with social justice.')
L(2,14,'2.2.1 Concept of Economic Planning','Economic planning and Five Year Plans','Economic planning is the deliberate allocation of resources according to a defined set of objectives over a specified period. India launched its First Five Year Plan in 1951. Planning sought coordinated development rather than leaving the allocation of scarce resources entirely to market forces.\n\nDistinguish a plan’s common long-term goals from the priorities of a particular plan. Agriculture, industry and infrastructure received different emphasis at different stages. This chapter describes the historical planning period 1950–1990.')
L(2,14,'2.2.2 Common Goals of Five Year Plans','Growth, modernisation, self-reliance and equity','Growth: increase the economy’s productive capacity and its output of goods and services. Modernisation: adopt new technology and change social outlook, including greater recognition of women’s participation. Self-reliance: reduce dependence on foreign resources, particularly for food and essential production. Equity: ensure that the benefits of development reach all sections and basic needs are met.\n\nThese goals can create trade-offs. Growth alone does not guarantee equity; modern machinery can raise productivity while reducing labour requirements. A good answer defines the goal and adds its purpose, rather than treating all four as synonyms.')
L(2,11,'2.3.2 Land Reforms [Institutional Reforms]','Land reforms: changing agrarian relations','Abolition of intermediaries aimed to bring cultivators into direct contact with the government and reduce exploitation. Land ceiling fixed a maximum permitted holding, allowing surplus land to be redistributed. Consolidation brought scattered plots together to improve efficiency. Cooperative farming pooled resources while retaining individual ownership.\n\nImplementation was uneven: loopholes, litigation and transfers to relatives weakened ceilings; not all tenants secured ownership. Institutional reforms change ownership and tenure; technical reforms change methods and inputs. Keep these headings separate in an answer.')
L(2,11,'2.3.3 Technical Reforms: HYV Technology and Green Revolution','The Green Revolution package','Green Revolution refers to a substantial increase in agricultural production through the use of high-yielding variety seeds and complementary inputs. The package required irrigation, chemical fertilisers, pesticides, credit and improved practices. HYV seeds alone were insufficient.\n\nIn its first phase, the gains were concentrated in wheat-growing regions with reliable irrigation, such as Punjab, Haryana and western Uttar Pradesh. The second phase spread the technology to more crops and regions. The initial regional concentration is central to questions about inequality.')
L(2,10,'2.3.5 Gains of Green Revolution','Green Revolution: gains','Increase in foodgrain production reduced dependence on imports and helped achieve food security. Higher productivity generated a marketable surplus—the portion sold after the farmer’s own needs. Better supplies helped stabilise food prices and enabled buffer stocks. Rising farm incomes stimulated demand for industrial inputs and consumer goods.\n\nMarketable surplus matters because food must reach consumers outside agriculture. An increase in total production does not necessarily raise marketed output by the same amount.')
L(2,9,'2.3.6 Limitations of Green Revolution','Green Revolution: limitations','Limited crop coverage: initial gains mainly benefited wheat and rice. Regional imbalance: areas with irrigation and supporting infrastructure gained more. Personal inequality: larger farmers could afford the input package more easily. Ecological costs: excessive chemical use and groundwater extraction damaged soil, water and sustainability.\n\nUse balanced appraisal: higher food output was a major achievement, but its distribution and environmental costs require separate assessment. The small farmer’s difficulty was access to complementary resources, not merely unwillingness to adopt seeds.')
L(2,9,'2.3.7 Agricultural Subsidies: Vice or Virtue?','The subsidy debate','Arguments in favour: subsidies encourage adoption of new technology, reduce farmers’ costs, support small cultivators and help stabilise food supplies. Arguments against: benefits can accrue disproportionately to prosperous farmers and input suppliers; subsidies burden government finances and may encourage wasteful use of fertilisers, electricity and water.\n\nA reasoned answer can support targeted assistance while questioning indiscriminate continuation. Preserve both sides of the textbook heading “Vice or Virtue?” rather than presenting subsidies as wholly good or bad.')
L(2,8,'2.4.1 Public and Private Sectors in Indian Industrial Development','Why the public sector led industrialisation','Private entrepreneurs lacked sufficient capital for many basic and heavy industries, and long gestation periods reduced their willingness to invest. Government investment supplied infrastructure and strategic industries, encouraged balanced regional development and pursued social welfare.\n\nThe public sector’s leading role did not abolish private enterprise. It supported the mixed-economy strategy, although protection and weak competitive pressure could later contribute to inefficiency.')
L(2,7,'2.4.2 Industrial Policy Resolution, 1956','IPR 1956 and industrial licensing','The IPR 1956 classified industries into three categories: industries exclusively owned by the state; industries in which the state would take the leading role with private participation; and remaining industries open to private enterprise subject to regulation. Industrial licensing controlled establishment and expansion.\n\nLicences could be used to promote industries in backward regions. However, excessive controls also limited entry, expansion and competition. State the policy’s intended role before evaluating its limitations.')
L(2,7,'2.4.3 Small-Scale Industry','Small-scale industries and the Karve Committee','The Karve Committee (1955) recognised the importance of small-scale industries for rural development. Their labour-intensive character generated employment with relatively little capital, encouraged decentralisation and supported more equitable income distribution.\n\nProtection included reservation of certain products, concessions and access to credit. Their limitations included lack of finance, outdated technology and difficulties in marketing. For a question about the rationale for protection, connect protection to employment, regional balance and the survival of small producers.')
L(2,6,'2.5 Trade Policy: Import Substitution','Import substitution, tariffs and quotas','Import substitution means replacing imports with domestic production. Tariffs are taxes on imports, making them more expensive. Quotas impose quantitative restrictions on imports. Both protect domestic producers from foreign competition and can conserve foreign exchange.\n\nProtection helped establish an industrial base, but prolonged shelter reduced incentives to improve quality, cut costs and innovate. Self-reliance and self-sufficiency are related but distinct: relying on domestic capacity does not require producing every good domestically.')
L(2,5,'2.6 India’s Growth Strategy during 1950–1990','Appraising the pre-reform strategy','Characteristic features: public-sector leadership, regulated private enterprise, protection of small industries, import substitution and an inward-looking development strategy. Achievements included a diversified industrial base, infrastructure, higher agricultural output and reduced dependence for many essentials.\n\nLimitations included slow growth, inefficient public enterprises, restrictive licensing, inadequate employment creation and persistent poverty. Structure an appraisal as achievements followed by limitations; avoid saying the period produced no development simply because reforms became necessary.')
L(3,12,'3.1 Need for New Economic Policy','Why reforms became necessary in 1991','The crisis combined a mounting fiscal deficit, rising debt, inflation, weak returns from public enterprises and a severe balance-of-payments problem. Foreign-exchange reserves fell to a level barely sufficient for about two weeks of imports. India approached the World Bank and IMF for assistance.\n\nThe New Economic Policy aimed to create a more competitive environment and remove barriers to entry and growth. The shift was from an inward-looking strategy towards greater private participation and integration with the world economy. Separate immediate crisis-management from longer-term structural reform.')
L(3,12,'3.2 Elements of NEP · 3.2.1 Liberalisation','Liberalisation: meaning and industrial reforms','Liberalisation means freedom of producing units from direct or physical controls of the government. Industrial reforms reduced licensing requirements, reduced industries reserved for the public sector, removed many restrictions on expansion and encouraged competition.\n\nLiberalisation does not mean the complete absence of government: regulation for safety, competition and public welfare continues. Explain the mechanism: fewer unnecessary entry restrictions → greater competition and flexibility → incentives to improve efficiency.')
L(3,11,'3.2.1 Liberalisation · Financial Sector Reforms','Financial-sector reforms','Financial-sector reforms sought to increase competition and efficiency in banks and other financial institutions. The RBI’s role shifted towards facilitating financial activity while retaining regulation and safeguards. Private and foreign banks received greater scope; foreign institutional investment was encouraged under rules.\n\nDistinguish a facilitator from an institution with no oversight. Reforms reduced certain controls; they did not remove the need for prudential regulation or depositor protection.')
L(3,10,'3.2.1 Liberalisation · Fiscal and Tax Reforms','Tax reforms, GST and demonetisation','Tax reforms sought a simpler structure, rationalised rates, a wider tax base and better compliance. Direct taxes are imposed directly on income or wealth; indirect taxes apply to goods and services and may be passed on through prices. GST, introduced on 1 July 2017, subsumed many indirect taxes into a common framework.\n\nDemonetisation in November 2016 withdrew the legal-tender status of the then ₹500 and ₹1,000 notes. The textbook discusses formalisation, digital payments and the campaign against unaccounted money, along with disruption to cash-dependent activity. Distinguish intended objectives from proof that every objective was achieved.')
L(3,9,'3.2.1 Liberalisation · Foreign Exchange and Trade Reforms','Devaluation and trade reforms','In 1991 the rupee was devalued to address the external crisis and encourage exports. Trade reforms reduced quantitative restrictions and import tariffs and made export-import procedures simpler. The strategy aimed to expose domestic firms to international competition and improve access to technology and inputs.\n\nDevaluation is an official reduction in currency value under a managed/fixed-rate arrangement; depreciation is a fall arising through market forces. Cheaper exports in foreign-currency terms can improve competitiveness, while imports become costlier in rupees.')
L(3,9,'3.2.2 Privatisation','Privatisation and disinvestment','Privatisation involves a greater role for the private sector through transfer of ownership or management of public enterprises. Disinvestment means sale of government equity. It may raise revenue, broaden ownership and introduce commercial discipline.\n\nA minority sale does not necessarily transfer control: disinvestment and full privatisation are not identical. Criticisms include undervaluation of public assets, loss of future public income and possible job losses. Appraise efficiency gains against social and fiscal consequences.')
L(3,7,'3.2.2 Privatisation · Public Enterprise Autonomy','Autonomy and public-enterprise status','The textbook discusses greater managerial and financial autonomy for selected public enterprises, including Navratna and Maharatna arrangements. Autonomy allows commercial decisions with fewer routine controls and can improve efficiency.\n\nAutonomy is different from a sale: a public enterprise can receive more decision-making freedom while remaining government-owned. Company status and lists in textbook boxes are tied to the book’s date; learn the policy concept and use the original box for the names your class expects.')
L(3,6,'3.2.3 Globalisation','Globalisation and outsourcing','Globalisation means integration of the domestic economy with the world economy through trade, investment, technology and movement of services. Outsourcing means contracting particular tasks to an external provider, often located abroad.\n\nIndia attracted outsourcing because of skilled English-speaking labour, relatively low costs, communication technology and time-zone advantages. Examples include IT services, customer support, accounting and business-process services. Globalisation is the broad process; outsourcing is one manifestation of it.')
L(3,6,'3.2.3 Globalisation · World Trade Organisation','WTO: purpose and debate','The World Trade Organisation was established in 1995 as the successor to GATT. It provides a framework for multilateral trade rules and dispute settlement and seeks to reduce barriers and discriminatory trade practices.\n\nThe debate concerns unequal bargaining power, the ability of developing countries to protect vulnerable producers and the continuation of subsidies in richer countries. State both the intended rules-based trading role and the distributional concerns; do not equate WTO membership with the removal of every trade barrier.')
L(3,5,'3.3.1 Good Effect of LPG Policies','Reforms: favourable effects','Textbook headings include a vibrant economy, industrial production, fiscal deficit, foreign-exchange reserves, inflation, consumer sovereignty, foreign direct investment, emerging economic power and competition. Greater entry and foreign links expanded consumer choice, technology access and competitive pressure.\n\nUse headings selectively according to the question, and explain the link: competition → pressure to improve quality and efficiency; investment → finance and technology; wider choice → consumer sovereignty. Growth and reserves should be described with the period attached to the textbook data, not as permanent current trends.')
L(3,4,'3.3.2 Bad Effect of LPG Policies','Reforms: criticisms and uneven gains','Neglect of agriculture: reduced support and exposure to international competition affected farmers. Industrial slowdown: import competition and inadequate infrastructure constrained some firms. Unemployment: growth did not always generate sufficient secure jobs. Disinvestment and fiscal-policy concerns: asset sales and spending priorities could weaken social objectives.\n\nOther textbook headings include lopsided growth, concentration in urban areas and economic dependence/consumerism. A balanced appraisal recognises gains while examining who benefited. Liberalisation does not guarantee equal gains across sectors, regions and income groups.')
L(4,1,'4.1.1 Human Resource · 4.1.2 Human Capital Formation','Human resource, human capital and formation','Human resource refers to the abilities—skill and expertise—of human beings to contribute to value addition. Human capital is the stock of knowledge, skills and health embodied in people. Human capital formation is the process of adding to that stock through education, training and health.\n\nA population is not automatically a highly productive resource: capabilities matter. Human capital is a stock at a point in time; expenditure that adds to it is a flow over a period.')
L(4,1,'4.1.3 Human Capital vs. Human Development','Human capital versus human development','Human capital treats education and health as means of raising productivity and income. Human development treats the enhancement of people’s well-being, freedom and quality of life as an end in itself.\n\nThe human-development view supports education and health even when the immediate productivity gain is small. For a distinction question use parallel bases: purpose, treatment of education/health, and the importance of well-being. Avoid saying human development is merely a means to economic growth.')
L(4,2,'4.1 Physical Capital vs. Human Capital','Physical capital versus human capital','Physical capital is tangible and separable from its owner; human capital is intangible and inseparable from the person. Physical capital can be sold as an asset, while a person sells the services of human capital. Mobility of human capital is restricted by nationality, language and culture.\n\nBoth can depreciate. Human capabilities decline through ageing or outdated skills, but continuing education, training and health expenditure can counter this. Human capital generates private economic returns and wider social benefits, such as informed citizenship and reduced spread of disease.')
L(4,2,'4.2 Sources of Human Capital','Education and health expenditure','Education improves knowledge, skills and future earning capacity. Health expenditure improves efficiency and productive working time. Textbook components include preventive medicine, curative medicine, social medicine/health awareness, hygiene and sanitation.\n\nThese are investments because they create benefits over time. An educated but unhealthy worker may be unable to use acquired skills fully; the sources complement one another. For theory answers name the source, explain how it raises capability and give an example.')
L(4,5,'4.2 Sources of Human Capital','Training, migration and information','On-the-job training develops job-specific skills through workplace learning or formal programmes. Migration involves costs of travel, settlement and sometimes a higher cost of living, undertaken in expectation of better employment and earnings. Information expenditure helps people identify suitable education and employment opportunities.\n\nThe textbook also discusses adult education/study programmes for upgrading skills. Distinguish the investment cost from its expected return: a migrant’s fare is a cost; the later increase in wages is a return.')
L(4,5,'4.3 Human Capital Formation and Economic Growth','How human capital contributes to growth','Higher productivity: skilled and healthy people produce more efficiently. Better use of physical capital: workers can operate advanced equipment. Innovation: education supports scientific and technological progress. Greater participation and social change: education widens opportunities and improves decision-making.\n\nHuman capital and growth reinforce one another: growth provides resources for education and health, which then support further growth. Measuring years of schooling alone is insufficient because quality and usable skills also matter.')
L(4,4,'4.4 Problems Facing Human Capital Formation in India','Problems of human capital formation','Rapid population growth spreads available resources thinly. Brain drain moves skilled people abroad. Deficient manpower planning can produce a mismatch between qualifications and jobs. Insufficient on-the-job training limits practical capability. Low academic standards and inadequate basic education reduce the quality of learning.\n\nUse the textbook’s headings and connect each problem to capability or productivity. More qualifications do not automatically mean better human capital when teaching quality and relevant skills are weak.')
L(4,3,'4.5.1 Importance and Objectives of Education','Education: wider objectives','Education creates responsible citizens, develops science and technology, enables effective use of resources, broadens horizons and supports growth. It also transmits culture, develops personality and improves participation in society.\n\nThese benefits explain why education has both private and social returns. In a human-development question, include dignity, opportunity and quality of life; in a growth question, emphasise skills, productivity and innovation.')
L(4,3,'4.5.2 Need for Government Intervention','Why government intervenes in education and health','Large investment requirements, high private prices, unequal ability to pay, the need for standards and social benefits justify government involvement. Provision and regulation can widen access and prevent poor-quality services. Education and health are too important to depend entirely on household purchasing power.\n\nExpenditure as a percentage of GDP and expenditure as a percentage of total government spending use different denominators. The Kothari Commission recommended education spending of 6% of GDP. The Right to Education Act, 2009 provides free and compulsory education for children aged 6–14.')
L(4,7,'4.5 Education Sector: Achievements and Challenges','Achievements, knowledge economy and remaining gaps','Expansion of schools and higher education, improvements in literacy and development of technical skills helped India participate in a knowledge economy. NCERT, UGC and AICTE are among the institutions discussed for educational development and standards; use the original pages for their particular roles.\n\nChallenges include illiteracy, inadequate vocationalisation, gender and rural–urban inequality, high private costs and insufficient public expenditure. Access, quality and employability require separate attention. Historical tables show changes over specific years and should not be quoted as live statistics.')
L(5,12,'5.1 Rural Development: Meaning and Significance','Rural development and the action plan','Rural development is a comprehensive process of improving the economic and social conditions of people in rural areas. It includes human resources, land reforms, productive resources, infrastructure and poverty alleviation. Its significance follows from the large rural population and dependence on agriculture.\n\nAn action plan should connect needs to measures: education and health; irrigation and roads; affordable credit; effective marketing; diversification into non-farm work; and sustainable use of resources. Rural development is broader than increasing crop production.')
L(5,11,'5.2.1 Rural Credit','Why rural credit is needed','Farmers need short-term credit for seeds, fertilisers, wages and consumption between harvests, and long-term credit for land improvement, machinery and irrigation. The gap between expenditure and harvest receipts creates seasonal borrowing needs.\n\nInstitutional sources include cooperative credit societies, commercial banks, regional rural banks and land-development banks. Non-institutional sources include moneylenders, traders, landlords and relatives. Institutional credit aims to reduce dependence on exploitative lending, but access and repayment problems remain.')
L(5,10,'5.2.1 Rural Credit · NABARD and Micro-credit','NABARD, self-help groups and micro-credit','NABARD, established in 1982, is the apex institution for coordinating rural credit and supporting agriculture and rural development. Self-help groups pool small savings and provide loans to members, often linking with banks. Micro-credit can reach people who lack conventional collateral.\n\nBenefits include financial inclusion, women’s empowerment and reduced dependence on moneylenders. Limitations include small loan size, uneven coverage and insufficient support for larger productive investment. A loan is useful when combined with viable activity, skills and market access.')
L(5,9,'5.2.1 Rural Credit · Problems','Weaknesses of rural credit','Inadequate and unequal access leaves small and marginal farmers dependent on informal lenders. Delays, paperwork and collateral requirements can exclude borrowers. Overdues and weak recovery affect lending institutions, while unproductive borrowing and crop failure make repayment difficult.\n\nSeparate borrower-side difficulties from institutional difficulties. The solution is not simply “more loans”: timely credit, suitable terms, productive use, insurance and effective recovery also matter.')
L(5,8,'5.2.2 Agricultural Marketing','Agricultural marketing and its defects','Agricultural marketing includes assembling, processing, grading, packaging, storing, transporting and selling farm produce. Defects include distress sales, lack of storage, faulty weights, inadequate market information, poor transport and excessive intermediary margins.\n\nA farmer may sell cheaply immediately after harvest because of urgent debt and no storage, even if prices rise later. This links the credit and marketing problems. Marketing is the entire movement from producer to consumer, not only the final sale.')
L(5,7,'5.2.2 Agricultural Marketing · Remedial Measures','Improving agricultural marketing','Regulated markets aim to make transactions fair. Infrastructure—storage, cold chains, roads, transport and information—reduces losses and improves bargaining. Cooperative marketing pools output and strengthens farmers’ negotiating position. Government measures include minimum support prices, procurement, buffer stocks and the public distribution system.\n\nDirect marketing reduces intermediaries: examples in the textbook include Apni Mandi, Rythu Bazaars and Uzhavar Sandies. Contract arrangements can provide assured markets and inputs, but unequal bargaining power and contract enforcement remain concerns.')
L(5,6,'5.3.1 Diversification of Productive Activities','Why rural diversification matters','Diversification includes changing the cropping pattern and expanding into allied agricultural and non-farm activities. It reduces dependence on seasonal crop farming, spreads risk and creates employment and supplementary income. It also offers work to people disguisedly employed in agriculture.\n\nExamples include animal husbandry, fisheries, horticulture, food processing, crafts and rural services. Diversification into cash crops needs a balance with food security and environmental sustainability.')
L(5,5,'5.3.1 Diversification · Animal Husbandry and Fisheries','Animal husbandry, fisheries and Operation Flood','Animal husbandry provides milk, meat, eggs and other products and can supplement crop income throughout the year. Operation Flood developed milk cooperatives, linked producers to consumers and supported the White Revolution. Better breeds, veterinary care, feed and marketing improve productivity.\n\nFisheries offer income and employment but face shortages of finance, equipment and infrastructure, along with pollution and ecological risks. Women contribute substantially to processing and marketing. Allied activities require support services; diversification alone does not remove vulnerability.')
L(5,4,'5.3.1 Diversification · Horticulture and IT','Horticulture and information technology','Horticulture includes fruits, vegetables, flowers and plantation crops. It can raise incomes, improve nutrition and create employment in processing, packaging and marketing. Its perishable output requires cold storage, reliable transport and market access.\n\nInformation technology improves access to weather forecasts, prices, farming advice and employment information. It can support rural services and reduce information gaps, but electricity, connectivity and digital skills determine whether people can benefit.')
L(5,3,'5.3.2 Organic Farming','Organic farming: meaning and advantages','Organic farming relies on organic inputs and ecological methods rather than synthetic fertilisers and pesticides. It supports soil health, reduces chemical pollution and can lower dependence on purchased chemical inputs. Demand for organic produce may provide premium prices and export opportunities.\n\nOther benefits include healthier environments and employment in labour-intensive practices. Avoid claiming that every organic product is automatically more nutritious or that every farmer receives a premium: outcomes depend on production, certification and markets.')
L(5,2,'5.3.2 Organic Farming · Limitations and Support','Organic farming: constraints and the PRANAM box','Transition may bring lower yields, and produce can have a shorter shelf life. Farmers need training, suitable organic inputs, certification, infrastructure and reliable marketing. Premium prices are difficult to obtain without consumer trust and access to suitable buyers.\n\nThe supplied PRANAM box concerns promoting alternative nutrients and reducing excessive chemical-fertiliser use. Learn its link with soil health and sustainable input use; it is not the definition of organic farming. For an appraisal give advantages and constraints under separate headings.')
L(6,13,'6.1.1 Who is a Worker? · 6.1.2 Workers and Employment','Workers and economic activity','A worker is a person engaged in an economic activity contributing to production. Workers include self-employed people and hired employees; payment need not always take the form of a regular salary. Unpaid family workers participating in a family enterprise can count as workers.\n\nHousehold services performed for one’s own family are generally outside the measured economic workforce. Distinguish being busy from participating in counted economic activity. Do not exclude a family farm helper simply because no separate wage is paid.')
L(6,13,'6.1.3 Self-employed and Hired Workers','Self-employed, regular and casual workers','Self-employed workers own and operate their enterprises or work on their own account. Regular salaried employees work for employers on a continuing basis and receive regular remuneration. Casual wage labourers are hired for short periods or particular tasks and face less security.\n\nExamples: a shop owner is self-employed; a permanent school employee is regular salaried; a daily-wage construction labourer is casual. Employment status describes the relationship to the job; it is separate from the sector producing the output.')
L(6,11,'6.1.4 Labour Supply, Labour Force, Workforce','Labour force, workforce and participation','Labour force includes people working and those available and willing to work who are seeking work. Workforce includes only the employed. Labour supply concerns the labour services or hours offered at a wage. A person outside the labour force is not automatically unemployed.\n\nWorker-population ratio measures the percentage of the population that is employed. The unemployment rate uses labour force as its denominator. Never interchange these denominators.','Labour force = Workforce + Unemployed\nWorker-population ratio = Workers ÷ Population × 100\nUnemployment rate = Unemployed ÷ Labour force × 100')
L(6,12,'6.2 Size of Workforce · 6.3 Rate of Participation','Rural–urban and male–female participation','Rural participation can be high because poverty compels work and farming offers scope for family labour. Urban participation reflects longer education, different job structures and fewer subsistence opportunities. Female participation is affected by social attitudes, care responsibilities, access to jobs and under-recording of economic work.\n\nA lower measured female participation rate does not mean women make no economic contribution. Interpret the textbook’s gender and regional tables using their stated year and definition; do not assume one figure applies to every period.')
L(6,10,'6.5 Occupational Structure or Distribution of Workforce','Workforce across sectors','Primary activities include agriculture and allied work; secondary activities include manufacturing and construction; tertiary activities include trade, transport and other services. Rural workers are more concentrated in agriculture, while urban workers are more concentrated in secondary and tertiary activities.\n\nEconomic development normally involves movement towards more productive non-farm work. If agriculture’s employment share is much larger than its output share, average output per worker is relatively low. A sector’s contribution to GDP and its share of workers are distinct indicators.')
L(6,7,'6.6 Jobless Growth','Jobless growth and technology','Jobless growth describes growth of output without a corresponding adequate expansion in employment. Capital-intensive technology, productivity improvements and limited expansion of labour-intensive activities can make GDP rise faster than jobs.\n\nIt does not necessarily mean employment is literally unchanged in every year. Assess the gap between output growth and employment growth. Policy responses include skills, labour-intensive manufacturing, rural diversification and support for small enterprises.')
L(6,6,'6.7.1 Casualisation','Casualisation of the workforce','Casualisation is movement from self-employment and regular salaried work towards casual wage employment. It increases job insecurity and often reduces access to employment benefits. Migration of surplus rural labour and weak bargaining power can contribute.\n\nUse data carefully: the definition is not proof that the casual-worker share rose in every period. The textbook’s table contains periods of decline as well as increase. Quote the actual pair of years when interpreting a trend.')
L(6,6,'6.7.2 Informalisation','Formal and informal employment','The textbook defines the formal sector as government departments, public enterprises and private establishments hiring ten or more workers. The informal sector includes smaller private enterprises, farming and self-employment ventures. Formal workers generally have better protection and social-security benefits.\n\nInformalisation means an increasing share of work takes place without the protection and benefits associated with formal employment. Casualisation concerns employment status; informalisation concerns organisation and protection. They can overlap, but they are not identical.')
L(6,4,'6.8 Unemployment · 6.8.1 Types','Open, disguised and seasonal unemployment','Open unemployment occurs when a person willing and able to work cannot obtain employment. Disguised unemployment occurs when more people are engaged than required: some can be removed without reducing output, so their marginal productivity is zero. Seasonal unemployment occurs when work is available only during particular seasons.\n\nEducated unemployment concerns qualified people unable to find suitable work; structural unemployment reflects a mismatch between labour skills or location and available jobs. In a family-farm example, check whether output changes when workers leave before calling it disguised unemployment.')
L(6,3,'6.8.2 Causes of Unemployment in India','Why unemployment persists','Slow employment-generating growth and rapid labour-force expansion create a gap between job demand and supply. Seasonal agriculture, inadequate irrigation, limited non-farm opportunities, decay of traditional industries, insufficient investment and low labour mobility contribute.\n\nEducation–job mismatch and capital-intensive production can worsen the problem. Present each heading with its mechanism: lack of irrigation → one crop season → months without farm work. A list without explanation is weaker than distinct explained points.')
L(6,2,'6.9 Government and Employment Generation','Government measures and MGNREGA','Direct employment comes from government departments and public enterprises. Indirect employment arises when public investment and policies stimulate private activity. Infrastructure, skills, small enterprises and rural development can expand opportunities.\n\nMGNREGA, 2005 provides a legal guarantee of at least 100 days of wage employment in a financial year to a rural household whose adult members volunteer for unskilled manual work. The entitlement is per household, not per adult. The textbook discusses provision of work within 15 days of demand and unemployment allowance if eligible work is not provided; at least one-third of beneficiaries should be women.')
