M(1,'Statement I: Agriculture dominated colonial employment. Statement II: This necessarily implied high agricultural productivity.', ['Both are true','Both are false','I is true; II is false','I is false; II is true'],2,'High dependence on agriculture coexisted with low productivity and stagnation.','Two statements')
M(1,'Which is a cause of agricultural stagnation under British rule?', ['Universal ownership rights for tenants','Heavy investment in irrigation','Exploitative land settlement','Abundant institutional credit'],2,'Insecure tenure and high rent discouraged investment, alongside low technology and inadequate irrigation.')
M(1,'De-industrialisation means:', ['Growth of every manufacturing industry','Decline of handicrafts without adequate modern industrial replacement','A fall in agricultural employment only','A ban on all imports'],1,'Artisans lost livelihoods while modern industry grew too slowly to absorb them.')
M(1,'1921 is called the Year of the Great Divide in the context of:', ['Industrial licensing','Population growth','GST','Public-sector disinvestment'],1,'It marks the transition from irregular/stagnant population growth towards sustained growth.')
M(1,'Commercialisation of agriculture primarily concerns:', ['Producing for market sale','Producing only for family consumption','Abolishing land revenue','Replacing every crop with machinery'],0,'Its defining feature is production for sale; improved farmer welfare does not automatically follow.')
M(1,'Assertion: Railways had some beneficial effects on India. Reason: Their primary colonial objective was Indian social welfare.', ['Both true; reason explains assertion','Both true; reason does not explain assertion','Assertion true; reason false','Assertion false; reason true'],2,'Transport and communication benefits existed, but colonial strategic and commercial interests were the primary motive.','Assertion–reason')
M(1,'Occupational structure measures:', ['Distribution of workers across activities','Distribution of tax rates','Distribution of government debt','Only exports by commodity'],0,'It concerns where people work, rather than sectors’ GDP shares.')
M(1,'The decline of royal courts mainly weakened:', ['Demand for traditional handicrafts','The supply of British machine-made imports','The government’s GST collections','Modern service exports'],0,'Royal patronage had supported specialised handicrafts.')
M(2,'Which is NOT one of the four common goals of planning?', ['Growth','Equity','Modernisation','Permanent dependence on imports'],3,'The fourth goal is self-reliance.')
M(2,'Replacing imports with domestically produced goods is:', ['Disinvestment','Import substitution','Outsourcing','Informalisation'],1,'An inward-looking strategy supported domestic industry through protection.')
M(2,'A quota differs from a tariff because a quota:', ['Is a tax on income','Sets a quantitative limit on imports','Always makes imports free','Is only a subsidy'],1,'A tariff is a tax on imports; a quota restricts their quantity.')
M(2,'Abolition of intermediaries is an example of:', ['Technical reform','Institutional land reform','Foreign-exchange reform','A monetary-policy instrument'],1,'It changes agrarian ownership and tenure relations.')
