def W(n,t,q,sol,marks=4,tag='Application'):
 data['questions'].append(dict(id='ied-written-'+str(sum(x['id'].startswith('ied-written') for x in data['questions'])+1),chapter='i'+str(n),type=t,marks=marks,q=q,solution=sol,tag=tag))
W(6,'numerical','A village has 2,000 people, 900 employed people and 100 unemployed people seeking work. Calculate its labour force, worker-population ratio and unemployment rate.','Labour force = 900 + 100 = 1,000.\nWorker-population ratio = 900/2,000 ×100 = 45%.\nUnemployment rate = 100/1,000 ×100 = 10%.\nThe two rates use different denominators.')
W(6,'numerical','A region’s population is 5 lakh and its worker-population ratio is 40%. Its labour force is 2.5 lakh. Calculate workers, unemployed people and the unemployment rate.','Workers = 5 ×0.40 = 2 lakh.\nUnemployed = 2.5 −2 = 0.5 lakh.\nUnemployment rate = 0.5/2.5 ×100 = 20%.')
W(2,'numerical','A farm produces 120 quintals of wheat, retaining 35 quintals for family consumption and seed requirements. Find the marketable surplus.','Marketable surplus = Total output − Retained requirements.\n= 120 −35 = 85 quintals.\nMarketable surplus is not total farm production.',3)
W(4,'numerical','Education spending is ₹60 crore, GDP is ₹1,000 crore and total government expenditure is ₹300 crore. Express education spending as a percentage of each denominator.','Share of GDP = 60/1,000 ×100 = 6%.\nShare of government spending = 60/300 ×100 = 20%.\nBoth describe the same spending, but they answer different questions.',3)
W(6,'numerical','An economy produces output worth 1,000 units, of which agriculture contributes 200. Agriculture employs 50 of the economy’s 100 workers. Compare average output per worker in agriculture and the rest of the economy.','Agriculture: 200/50 = 4 output units per worker.\nOther sectors: (1,000−200)/(100−50) = 800/50 = 16 units per worker.\nAverage output per worker outside agriculture is four times as high. Employment shares and output shares are not interchangeable.')
W(1,'theory','“Railways prove that colonial economic policy primarily aimed to develop India.” Evaluate this statement.','Colonial infrastructure: purpose and effects\nThe statement confuses beneficial effects with the primary objective.\nRailways moved raw materials, expanded markets for British manufactures and served military/administrative needs.\nThey also improved transport, movement of people and commercial links.\nThese benefits should be acknowledged, but colonial interests determined priorities and design.',4,'Evaluate a statement')
W(2,'theory','A small farmer cannot adopt HYV seeds because irrigation and affordable credit are missing. Explain why distributing seeds alone may fail, and identify two suitable measures.','Green Revolution: complementary inputs\nHYV technology requires irrigation, fertilisers, plant protection and supporting finance. Seeds alone cannot secure the expected yield.\nExpand reliable irrigation and provide timely institutional credit on suitable terms.\nTraining and access to inputs/marketing can support adoption.\nTargeting small farmers can reduce unequal gains.',4,'Case-based')
W(3,'theory','The government sells 20% of its equity in an enterprise but retains control. Another public enterprise receives greater managerial autonomy. Are both necessarily privatisation? Explain.','Disinvestment, privatisation and autonomy\nThe equity sale is disinvestment. A minority sale with government control retained is not necessarily full privatisation.\nManagerial autonomy increases freedom to make decisions; it does not itself transfer ownership.\nPrivatisation involves a greater private role through ownership or management transfer.\nIdentify the actual change in control rather than relying only on the word reform.',4,'Case-based')
W(4,'theory','A health programme is justified solely by its effect on worker productivity. Explain the human-capital view and add the human-development perspective.','Human capital versus human development\nThe human-capital view treats health as an investment improving efficiency, attendance and productive earnings.\nThe human-development view regards health and a better quality of life as ends in themselves.\nIt supports health access even when immediate measured productivity gains are limited.\nThe perspectives overlap in valuing health, but differ in the ultimate purpose.',4,'Case-based')
W(5,'theory','A farmer sells immediately after harvest at a low price to repay a moneylender. Explain the linked credit and marketing problems, and suggest three measures.','Rural credit and agricultural marketing\nImmediate repayment needs and inadequate storage create a distress sale, weakening bargaining power.\nTimely affordable institutional credit can reduce dependence on the moneylender.\nStorage and warehouse facilities allow the farmer to delay sale.\nCooperative/direct marketing, better price information and fair regulated transactions improve returns.\nExplain how each measure addresses a specific difficulty.',6,'Case-based')
W(6,'theory','Output rises rapidly while secure jobs grow slowly and more workers take daily-wage work. Identify and distinguish the two processes.','Jobless growth and casualisation\nJobless growth: output increases without adequate corresponding employment growth. Capital-intensive techniques can contribute.\nCasualisation: movement towards casual wage work rather than regular salaried work or self-employment.\nThe first compares output and job growth; the second concerns employment status and security.\nDo not assume they are identical or that every casual worker is openly unemployed.',4,'Case-based')
